title: Critical Points
keyword: AUTO
manual: cpsearch/#c2-auto
cite: critic2_2014 critic2_2009

Finds the critical points (CPs) of a scalar field, the points where its gradient vanishes,
shows them in the view and lists their properties, as the AUTO keyword does. Choose the
"Field" at the top; its CPs are what the three tabs search, inspect and export.

# Search tab

- "Single shot": "Pick" searches for one CP from a point clicked in the view (the middle of
  a bond, or the clicked point) and adds it to the list.
- Seeds: the points the search starts from. Each entry adds a set of seeds of one kind
  (Wigner-Seitz subdivision, atom pairs, triplets, line, sphere, octahedron, point, mesh).
  "Add Seeds" adds an entry, "Reset Seeds" restores the default seeding of AUTO, and
  "Show seeds" draws the seeds in the view.
- Run: "Discard the existing critical points" starts from the nuclei only instead of adding
  to the list; "Do not write the checkpoint" skips saving the CPs to <field file>.chk_cps,
  which the GUI reads when the field is loaded again. More shows the AUTO thresholds
  (GRADEPS, CPEPS, NUCEPS, NUCEPSH, EPSDEGEN), DISCARD and CLIP. "Run" launches the search;
  "Estimate Cost" times a sample of the seeds.

# Results tab

The table lists the symmetry-unique CPs or all the CPs in the cell ("List") with their type,
coordinates, field value, derivatives, bond-path data and the point properties of the
system (added in "Point properties of the system"). Click a header to sort,
right-click it to choose columns. Hovering a row highlights the CP in the view; "All",
"None", "Toggle" and "Delete Selected" manage the selection. The counts per type and the
Morse sum are shown above it.

# Export tab

Writes the CPs to a file: JSON, a VMD script, or a structure file with the CPs as atoms.

# Notes

- After a run the CPs appear as the critical points object of the field in the view and the
  output goes to the Output console. "Clear" deletes all CPs but the nuclei.
- For grid fields with smoothrho interpolation an extra citation is printed in the output.
